meta {
  name: SYS-MED-02 a traversal path is refused
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/media/..%2f..%2f..%2fetc%2fpasswd/stream
  body: none
  auth: none
}

tests {
  test("SYS-MED-02 [CRITICAL]: the traversal is refused, not served", function() {
    expect([400, 404]).to.include(res.getStatus());
  });

  test("SYS-MED-02 [CRITICAL]: no file content leaks in the body", function() {
    const body = String(res.getBody() ?? "");
    expect(body).to.not.contain("root:");
  });
}

docs {
  Traces FR-SEC-06, NFR-SEC-03. Media is addressed by database id, never by a
  caller-supplied path, so this request should not resolve to a file under any
  circumstances. The second assertion exists because a 200 with a passwd file in
  it is the failure that matters; the status code alone is not the finding.

  Either 400 or 404 is acceptable: the id is malformed and it also does not
  exist, and arguing over which is more correct is not worth pinning in a test.
}
